    "content": "MBA students in the United States may require additional learning support, but the precise unmet needs, their frequency, and urgency are currently unknown and require further research. Similarly, the willingness of MBA students to pay for a dedicated AI tutor service is an unconfirmed assumption. Broader trends indicate that only 39% of respondents in the United States consider AI beneficial in education [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37], which could impact adoption. Furthermore, there is a significant adoption gap among higher education faculty, with only 22% regularly using generative AI compared to 83% of K-12 teachers [web-236a31a2f5a04bcb8b4ae6d39dee9e37]. Concerns about academic integrity are also prevalent, with 78% of teachers worried about students using AI to cheat and 66% having noticed an increase in plagiarism or AI misuse [web-22a440c045674c219e988a462c13f36c]. These factors highlight potential barriers to the successful integration and acceptance of an AI tutor in the MBA education landscape.",
